## 1. Introduction
The rapid development of communication technology, especially the emergence of 5G technology, marks that human society has begun to enter the era of the Internet of Everything, and the application of intelligent devices represented by gigahertz (GHz) frequency is woven into the fabric of our daily lives.[1] On the other hand, with such electromagnetic radiation all around us, health effects are a matter of concern.[2] Thus, electromagnetic wave (EMW) absorbing materials that possess high absorption, broad frequency, thin thickness, low filling, and flexible controllability have attracted more attention.[3] Conventional EMW absorbing materials, such as ferrite,[4] ceramics,[5] aerogels/sponges,[6] and metal oxides/sulfides,[7] exhibit excellent absorption efficiency but limited controllability. Excellent conductivity is generally a contributing factor to EMW absorption, making materials like MXenes, graphene, carbon nanotubes suitable choices. However, their excessively high conductivity can lead to serious skin effects and surface reflection, resulting in poor performance for EMW absorption.[8] Further, the critical factors retarding the advance of conventional microwave-absorption materials (MAMs) is the absence of precisely controllable atomically-resolved crystal structure, which hindered the understanding of the further post-synthetic modification.[9]
It remains a great conundrum to develop novel EMWabsorbing materials that can solve the abovementioned problems.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are promising candidates for tunable EMW-absorbing materials due to their aesthetic structures, fascinating customized functionality, large porosity, and high specific surface area.[10] However, the poor electrical conductivity of most pristine MOFs hinders their EMW absorption performance,[11] making the application of pristine MOFs for EMW absorption elusive. To improve the dielectric loss of MOFs, two strategies are usually adopted: carbonization of MOFs to carbon-based materials and composites of MOFs with conductive materials.[12] Unfortunately, carbonization at high temperatures and the confinement of conductive guests in the channels quickly cause the framework to collapse and reduce porosity. Furthermore, it is often impossible to predict the atomically-resolved structures of these MOF derivatives, as the uncertainties of pyrolysis and confinement further restrict the understanding of the structure-property relationship. Taking inspiration from the above discussion, pristine two-dimensional conjugated metal-organic frameworks (2D c-MOFs) may be the optimal candidate for EMW absorption. First, 2D c-MOFs are typically semiconductors,[13] with moderate electrical mobility allowing them to retain conduction loss without experiencing a strong skin effect. Second, the porous structure of MOFs is a natural feature that extends the reflection path of electromagnetic waves, thereby increasing absorption.[14] Thirdly, the combination of multiple metal ions, terminal functional groups, and grain boundaries generates abundant polarization loss. Lastly, the reticular synthesis of MOFs is favorable for improving EMW absorptioning performance by enlarging the electromagnetic losses and adjusting matching properties.
Although suitable 2D c-MOF have been selected,[15] their performance depends only on the inherent original properties, which is difficult to cope with complex and changeable practical applications. The synergistic effect of bimetals is recognized as a new avenue to ameliorate materials.[16] Due to the uniqueness of each metal atom, a reasonable combination of different metals not only can the synergistic effect between metals be exerted, but the parameters of a single crystal structure will be modified and then further regulate the contribution of the valence and conduction band density of states. These trimmings will effectively improve the electromagnetic performance and optimize the impedance matching coefficient.[17] While the structure of MOFs derivatives prepared by the bimetallic element mixed pyrolysis method generally exhibits single-phase ion doping or separated multiphase, making it tough to precisely regulate at the molecular level. Noteworthy, the heterometallic MOF allows the placement of various metals in different amounts with atomic precision within a porous framework simply by adjusting the ratio of metal elements.[18] This tunable feature of MOFs at the molecular level can be applied to achieve precise regulation of electromagnetic parameters.
To verify our assumption, a series of semiconducting 2D c-MOFs, $\mathbf{M}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ $(\mathrm{M}{=}\mathrm{C}_{})$ , Ni, Cu; $\mathrm{HITP}\ =\ 2,3,6,7,10,11.$ hexaimino triphenylene) have been successfully synthesized by a simple solvothermal method. [19] Due to their unique electrical properties, they have shown attractive potential in various fields,[20] but remain elusive in the field of EMW absorption. Herein, atomically resolved bimetallic $\mathrm{M_{x}M_{\bar{3}-X}^{\prime}(H I T P)_{2}}$ were constructed by a bimetallic alloying strategy. Atomic tuning of heterometallic MOFs leads to the alteration of free-carrier concentration and interlayer displacement or spacing, optimizing the electromagnetic properties thus endowing them with controllability toward EMW absorption. Profiting from the inherent electromagnetic loss and bimetallic synergistic self-polarization effect of HITP-MOFs, the minimum reflection loss (RL) value of $\mathrm{Cu}_{1.3}\mathrm{Ni}_{1.7}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ is up to $-71.5$ dB at only $2.1\ \mathrm{mm}$ of thickness, and the effective absorption bandwidth (EAB) of $6.16~\mathrm{GHz}$ $(11.84-18~\mathrm{GHz}$ ) covers the entire $\mathtt{K u}$ -band $(12{-}18~\mathrm{GHz})$ . Most of the MOF derivatives with excellent absorption properties are between $1.5{-}4.0~\mathrm{mm}$ in thickness, such as $\mathrm{Zn-C-Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ $_{-61.9}$ dB, $2.5\mathrm{\mm})$ , $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{y}\textcircled{a}C}$ $(-43.7$ dB, $1.7~\mathrm{mm};$ ,[21] and a few of the materials with less than $1.5~\mathrm{mm}$ thickness have relatively poor performance, such as S-700 (- 19 dB, $1.25~\mathrm{mm}$ ), S-60 (−39.2 dB, $1.4\mathrm{mm}$ ), and FCIP (−14 dB, $1\ \mathrm{mm}$ ).[22] Compared with MOF derivatives and even conventional absorbing materials, the comprehensive performance of $\mathrm{Cu}_{1.3}\mathrm{Ni}_{1.7}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ ranks among the top. Encouragingly, the elaborated control of the electromagnetic parameters and shift of absorption peak positions of $\mathrm{Cu_{x}N i_{3\cdot x}(H I T P)_{2}}$ can be achieved by adjusting the metal ratio. These findings demonstrate the comprehensive controllability of the pristine bimetallic MOF from composition, microstructure, and dielectric properties to EMW absorption properties. They also provide new insight into the potential of pristine conductive 2D MOFs as excellent candidates for highly efficient and tuned electromagnetic wave absorbers.
