## 2.2. Evaluation and Analysis of Electromagnetic Wave Adsorption Performance
## 2.2.1. Excellent Electromagnetic Wave Absorbing Performance
In general, the reflection loss (RL) values are used to evaluate the EMW absorption capability of a material, which can be calculated by Equations (1) and (2).[25] The frequency range $\scriptstyle({\mathrm{RL}}<-10$ dB) is defined as the effective absorption width (EAB). To better highlight the advantages of the bimetallic synergistic strategy, we plotted the 3D-RL and the corresponding 2D-RL diagrams of all MOFs for comparison. $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ (Figure 2a,d) and ${\mathrm{Co}}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ (Figure S8, Supporting Information) show nearly no performance with a filling of $15\%$ , the RL of $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ reaches $^{-37}$ dB in the thickness of $3.3~\mathrm{\mm}$ and EAB of $2.72\:\mathrm{GHz}$ (Figure 2b,e). Notably, with the introduction of bimetals, the EMW absorption performance improved by leaps and bounds. The RL values of $\mathrm{Cu}_{1.3}\mathrm{Ni}_{1.7}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ , $\mathrm{Co}_{0.7}\mathrm{Ni}_{2.3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{Cu}_{2.6}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ reached $-71.5$ , $-27.1$ , and $-61.8$ dB at a thin thickness of 2.1, 2.7, and $2.6\mathrm{mm}$ , respectively, and with the maximum EAB of 6.16, 4.44, and $1.16~\mathrm{GHz}$ (Figure 2c,f; Figure S8, Supporting Information). The EAB of $\mathrm{Cu}_{1.3}\mathrm{Ni}_{1.7}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ $(11.84\mathrm{-}18~\mathrm{GHz})$ ) covers the entire Ku-band ( $12{-}18~\mathrm{GHz})$ . Compared with $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ , $\mathrm{Cu}_{1.3}\mathrm{Ni}_{1.7}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ not only possesses a reduced absorption thickness (from 3.4 to $2.1\ \mathrm{mm}$ ), but an extended maximum EAB (from 2.72 to $6.16~\mathrm{GHz}$ ) and increased absorption strength (from $^{-37}$ to $-71.5$ dB). Moreover, the position of the effective absorption peak has also been adjusted to achieve a shift from the X-band to the Ku-band. Figure 2g,h further shows that the performance of $\mathrm{Cu}_{1.3}\mathrm{Ni}_{1.7}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ was a significant advance over that of monometallic MOFs in all aspects. The narrow absorption bandwidth and sharp absorption peak of $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ in X-band $(10{-}12\ \mathrm{GHz})$ indicate a preferred EMW absorption property. To retain and enlarge this characteristic, an appropriate amount of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ was introduced into $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ to optimize impedance matching, which prompts $\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{Cu}_{2.6}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ to have a more substantial absorption peak (from $^{-37}$ to $^{-61}$ dB) (Figure 2i). This property makes it a promising candidate for frequency-specific EMW absorption. Table 1 and Figure 4e show the comprehensive performance comparison of $\mathrm{Cu}_{1.3}\mathrm{Ni}_{1.7}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ and some reported MOFs and their derivatives. The RL value, EAB width, filling amount, and absorber thickness of $\mathrm{Cu}_{1.3}\mathrm{Ni}_{1.7}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ are shown to be ranked 1st, 2nd, 2nd, and fifth in four key indicators, respectively. Significantly, without high-temperature pyrolysis and complex postprocessing, $\mathrm{Cu}_{1.3}\mathrm{Ni}_{1.7}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ possesses the best comprehensive properties. To further reveal its absorption characteristics, we focus on two main aspects: electromagnetic loss and geometric morphology.
## 2.2.2. Large Dielectric Losses Derived from High Conduction Loss and Dielectric Polarization
According to the primary absorption mode of incident EMW, EMW-absorbing materials are generally divided into dielectric loss type and magnetic loss type, which can be reflected by the dielectric constant $(\epsilon^{\prime},\epsilon^{\prime\prime})$ and the magnetic permeability $(\mu^{\prime},\mu^{\prime\prime})$ , respectively. Among them, $\epsilon^{\prime}$ and $\mu^{\prime}$ are the real parts of the complex permittivity and the relative complex permeability constant, which represent the storage capacity of electrical polarization and magnetic polarization, respectively; in contrast, the imaginary parts of complex permittivity $(\epsilon^{\prime\prime})$ and complex permeability $(\mu^{\prime\prime})$ constant represent the loss ability of the material to electromagnetic waves when the electric dipole moment and the magnetic dipole moment are rearranged.[8] The electromagnetic parameters $(\epsilon^{\prime},\epsilon^{\prime\prime},\mu^{\prime},\mu^{\prime\prime})$ of all the MOFs were measured by vector network analysis in $2{\mathrm{-}}18\ {\mathrm{GHz}}$ at room temperature (Figure 3a,b;
Figure S10, Supporting Information), which indicates that the contributions of EMW absorption mainly come from conduction loss, polarization loss, hysteresis loss, and eddy current loss. The $\mu^{\prime}$ values of all MOFs are below 1.2, while $\mu^{\prime\prime}$ is less than 0.2, proving that they exhibit a relatively low ability to store and dissipate magnetic energy. On the contrary, the $\epsilon^{\prime}$ and $\epsilon^{\prime\prime}$ values of these MOFs vary between 4 to 14 and 1 to 4, respectively, which is consistent with the characteristics of dielectric loss type MEW absorbing materials.
To further verify our hypothesis, we tested the magnetic field strength at room temperature using a vibrating sample magnetometer (VSM) (Figure 3e). The order of the magnetic field strength is $\mathrm{Cu_{1.3}N i_{1.7}(H I T P)_{2}\mathrm{>Cu_{3}(H I T P)_{2}\mathrm{>Ni_{3}(H I T P)_{2}}}}$ . It shows that the introduction of bimetals improves the magnetic properties of MOFs, which may be due to the introduction of additional interaction between $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Ni^{2+}}$ with a distance of ${\approx}3.2\mathrm{\AA}.$ , thereby enhancing the magnetic. However, even though the magnetic field strength of $\mathrm{Cu}_{1.3}\mathrm{Ni}_{1.7}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ is $0.3\mathrm{\emu\g^{-1}}$ up to 6 times of 0.05 emu $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ , its maximum value is still too trivial compared to the general magnetic loss type materials.[26] The eddy current effect is based on the principle of electromagnetic induction and formed in the alternating magnetic field. The power loss caused by these currents is called eddy current loss. The $\mathbf{C}_{0}$ values (Equation (3)) of all samples in Figure 3d and Figure S11 (Supporting Information) fluctuated from $2{\mathrm{-}}18\ \mathrm{GHz}$ rather than staying horizontal, suggesting that in addition to eddy current losses, there may be magnetic losses due to natural and exchange resonances.[27] Natural resonance is typically located in the low GHz $(2{-}10\operatorname{GHz})$ , while resonance exchange generally occurs at a high frequency $(10{-}18\operatorname{GHz})$ . Furthermore, it can be recognized that in Figure 3d, natural resonance and exchange resonance are shown as the ups and downs of the curve. Compared to the eddy current effect, the resonance effect contributes relatively little to the magnetic loss. Almost all of the magnetic loss is the result of eddy current loss, so $\mathbf{C}_{0}$ is considered constant. Under this condition, the influence of eddy current on magnetic loss can be ignored.[28] Thus, the EMW absorption performance of these MOFs is mainly due to the dielectric loss, confirming our speculation.
Conduction loss and polarization loss are considered the major contributors to dielectric loss. The free electron theory indicates that there is a negative correlation between the resistance $\rho$ and the complex permittivity $\epsilon^{\prime\prime}$ $(\epsilon^{\prime\prime}\approx1/\pi\epsilon_{0}\rho\mathrm{f}_{\mathrm{:}}$ where $\epsilon_{0}$ is the free space impedance); a larger $\epsilon^{\prime\prime}$ will leads to a smaller $\rho$ and thus a larger conductivity.[29] Bimetallic MOFs have larger $\epsilon^{\prime}$ and $\epsilon^{\prime\prime}$ , which indicate better electrical conductivity. Notably, in $10-$ $12\ {\mathrm{GHz}}$ , the $\epsilon^{\prime\prime}$ of $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ has an apparent upward trend, and a peak appears at $11.32\mathrm{GHz}$ , which corresponds to the position of the maximum absorption peak of $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ . Similarly, the position of the maximum absorption peak corresponds to a large $\epsilon^{\prime\prime}$ in all the materials, means that there is a high correlation between absorption performance and conductivity, but a low correlation with permeability. In general, the dielectric loss dependents significantly on the conduction property and polarization relaxation. The conduction loss is caused by the jumping of electrons across the topological defect barriers and the transport of charges along the conductive channel, which can be reflected in the conductivity. We tested the conductivity by the dual probe method at room temperature, as shown in Figure 3f.
The unexpectedly low electrical conductivity of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ can be attributed to the general crystallinity in a large-scale synthesis. As expected, the enhancement in electrical conductivity of $\mathrm{Cu}_{1.3}\mathrm{Ni}_{1.7}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ is owing to the difference in interlayer spacing and interlayer displacement.[19] The electrical conductivity results indicate that the samples with higher conductivity usually manifest higher reflection loss.
To gain an in-depth understanding of carrier mobility and structure-property relationship, we performed theoretical calculations on the truncated model of the samples. Figures S12–S14a (Supporting Information) showed the optimized structures of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ , $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{Cu}_{1.3}\mathrm{Ni}_{1.7}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ . Density functional theory calculations reveal that the introduction of bimetals changes the electronic structure near the Fermi level $(\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{F}})$ . The total and partial density of states (Figures S12–S14b, Supporting Information) show the presence of partial waves crossing the Fermi level, proving that the material has semiconductor properties.[30] And the pseudogap (Figure 3h) of $\mathrm{Cu}_{1.3}\mathrm{Ni}_{1.7}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ is slightly wider, which indicates that the introduction of bimetals can increase the interaction leading to stable conductive channels. Moreover, by comparing the density of states maps of the metallic $d$ orbitals of MOFs in Figure $3\mathrm{h}$ , $\mathrm{Cu}_{1.3}\mathrm{Ni}_{1.7}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ has a larger density of states partial waves crossing the Fermi level, implying that it possesses the highest electron density and conductivity. Meanwhile, the differential charge density map shows the direction of electron transfer in the conduction channel inside the material. The blue and yellow parts represent charge accumulation and consumption, respectively. Additionally, the Bader charge analysis was used to calculate the charge transfer amount of these three MOFs. The results showed that $\mathrm{Cu}_{1.3}\mathrm{Ni}_{1.7}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ possess the largest charge transfer amount of $3.083\mathrm{e}$ , which corresponds to the highest conductivity (Figure 3i).
The Debye relaxation polarization theory is widely utilized to investigate dielectric polarization,[31] we can get the Cole-Cole semicircle through the Debye relaxation Equation (6). The $\epsilon^{\prime\prime}/\epsilon^{\prime}$ calculation models related to samples are shown in Figure $4\mathrm{a-d}$ and Figure S15 (Supporting Information). Compared to the semicircle number of 6 of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ and 5 of $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ , the semicircle number of $\mathrm{Cu}_{1.3}\mathrm{Ni}_{1.7}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ is up to 9, which means there are more polarization processes. This may be due to the dipole and interfacial polarization generated by bimetallic synergy, large surface area, and special microstructure. To further explore the effect of bimetallic synergy on dielectric polarization, we calculated the dipole moments of these MOFs (Figure S16, Supporting Information). The results showed that the introduction of metal nickel greatly enhanced the dipole moment of ${\mathrm{Cu-MOF}}$ , which means that it has a larger polarity and correspondingly more significant multiple polarization, without considering the polarization cancellation of high polarity local regions.
According to the principle of electromagnetic energy conversion,[32] the EMW-absorbing properties of materials are determined by not only the relative complex permeability $(\mu_{\mathrm{r}}=\mu^{\prime}-\mu^{\prime\prime})$ , relative complex permittivity $(\epsilon_{\mathrm{r}}=\epsilon^{\prime}-\epsilon^{\prime\prime})$ , but also by the intrinsic impedance ratio $(\boldsymbol{\mathsf{Z}})$ of these two parameters. The attenuation constant $(\alpha)$ is often used to describe the loss ability of the EMW absorbing material.[33] $Z$ and $\alpha$ can be calculated by Equations (4) and (5). The attenuation coefficient $(\alpha)$ of $\mathrm{Cu}_{1.3}\mathrm{Ni}_{1.7}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ is much larger than that of $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ in Figure 3c, indicating that it has more excellent absorption of incident waves. Similarly, the attenuation coefficients of bimetallic MOFs are more significant than those of monometallic MOFs (Figure S11, Supporting Information). It can be attributed to the enhanced dielectric and conductive polarization generated from the bimetallic synergistic effect discussed above.
2.2.3. Special Microstructure and Increased Specific Surface Area Facilitate Multi-Polarization and Scattering Effect
First, porous structures can reduce the density of materials, provide lightweight absorbers, and form cavities that can store air with close impedance matching with free space to ameliorate impedance matching.[34] Second, profuse channels and high specific surface area can improve the loss capacity, because it can achieve longer propagation paths and internal multiple scattering.[35]
In addition to the inherent electromagnetic properties of materials, geometric morphology and porous structure also play an important role in absorption performance. The results of the scanning electron microscope (SEM) and transmission electron microscope (TEM) revealed that $\mathbf{M}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ and $\mathrm{M_{x}M_{\bar{3}-X}^{\prime}(H I T P)_{2}}$ possess different morphology. In the SEM images, ${\mathrm{Co}}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ shows a spherical shape with a rough surface. $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ presents a uniform hexagonal prism shape, and $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ possesses a flower-like structure with uniform folds on the surface (Figure 5a–c). Interestingly, the bimetallic MOFs, $\mathrm{M_{x}M_{3-x}^{\prime}(H I T P)_{2}}$ , exhibit significantly different morphologies, wrinkled spherical for $\mathrm{Co}_{0.7}\mathrm{Ni}_{2.3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ , coralshaped for $\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{Cu}_{2.6}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ , and intertwined rod-shaped for $\mathrm{Cu}_{1.3}\mathrm{Ni}_{1.7}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ , (Figure 5d–f). It can be observed that the transformation of the micromorphology prompts closer contact with each unit, which contributes to the formation of heterointerfaces. The metal ions of $\mathrm{Co}_{0.7}\mathrm{Ni}_{2.3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ , $\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{Cu}_{2.6}$ $\left(\mathrm{HITP}\right)_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Cu}_{1.3}\mathrm{Ni}_{1.7}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ (Figure 5k-s) were uniformly distributed throughout the MOF particles as revealed by elemental mapping under a scanning electron microscope with energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy. In addition, the lattice fringes can be observed in the TEM images, indicating the high crystallinity of these MOFs (Figure $5\mathrm{g-j}$ ). Notably, these special morphology and microstructure of such MOFs are conducive to enhance the scattering effect due to the prolonged transmission distance of incident electromagnetic waves in the absorber by increasing the number of scattering and reflection,[36] leading to the multiple attenuation and energy conversion of induced current, thereby increasing electromagnetic losses. In addition, the high anisotropy of the rod-shape reduces the original permeation threshold and increases the cutoff frequency; the formed heterointerfaces lead to stronger interfacial polarization.[37]
The $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ adsorption of MOFs exhibited the type-I isotherm at 77 K (Figure 1). The Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface area of six MOFs were $259~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ , $451~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{Cu}_{2.6}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ , $474{\mathrm{~m}}^{2}{\mathrm{~g}}^{-1}$ for ${\mathrm{Co}}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ , 632 for $\mathbf{m}^{2}~\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{Co}_{0.3}\mathrm{Ni}_{2.7}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ , $469\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ , and $805\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{Cu}_{1.3}\mathrm{Ni}_{1.7}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ . The specific surface area and pore size of MOFs increased significantly with the introduction of bimetals. The reticulated structure with high surface-to-volume ratio not only has natural advantages in low density and high EMW attenuation geometric effects compared to conventional materials, but also provides more surface-active sites, allowing solid-air interfaces for interfacial polarization, which can enhance the polarization relaxation loss.[38] More importantly, the combination of profuse channels and high specific surface area will form a significant heterogeneous interface, which will further enhance EMW absorption through dipole/interface polarization.
## 2.2.4. Tunable Electromagnetic Wave Absorbing Performance and Loss Mechanisms
Considering that these MOFs are a dielectric loss type EMW absorption material, the electrical conductivity of MOFs can be tuned by the co-synergies of different metals and contents.[39]
Thus, we control the conduction loss capability of the materials by metal content design at the molecular level. A series of $\mathrm{Cu_{x}N i_{3\cdot x}(H I T P)_{2}}$ with $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}/\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ ratios of 1:4, 2:3, 1:1, 3:2, and 4:1 were synthesized, and PXRD analysis demonstrated the successful synthesis of the material (Figure S19, Supporting Information). The metal compositions of $\mathrm{Cu_{x}N i_{3-x}(H I T P)_{2}}$ were determined by ICP-MS and XPS (Figures S20–S23, Supporting Information). The comparison of the compositions determined by different methods with those of the precursors is summarized in Figure 6b. The actual measured value is close to our feed ratio, proving that the fine-tuning is accurate at the molecular level. The SEM image (Figure S24, Supporting Information) shows that these MOFs have a similar morphology to $\mathrm{Cu}_{1.3}\mathrm{Ni}_{1.7}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ , so the influence of microstructure can be excluded. Figure S25 (Supporting Information) shows the electrical conductivity of five samples with different $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}/\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ ratios; the conductivity does enhance with the increase of $\mathrm{Ni^{2+}}$ content, which is consistent with the conductivity enhancement trends reported in the literature.[19]
To further explore its controllability, we calculated impedance matching and attenuation coefficients at each scale (Figure S26, Supporting Information). Notably, the incompatibility between high conductivity and impedance matching requires MAMs to seek a perfect balance between them. Hence, $\mathrm{Cu}_{0.8}\mathrm{Ni}_{2.2}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ shows the highest conductivity but with a lower attenuation coefficient than $\mathrm{Cu}_{1.3}\mathrm{Ni}_{1.7}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ , which maintains an ideal balance resulting in better performance. Through the bimetallic molecular design strategy, we can control these decisive parameters that determine the performance over a wide range and select the materials with the best attenuation and impedance balance rather than rudderless traditional materials. This finding means that we can precisely tune the conduction loss and electromagnetic parameters of materials by a feasible design method in an unconventional but simple manner, thereby locking in optimal performance. Undoubtedly, it will be a critical leap in the field toward the customizable functionalization step. The EMW properties of the five bimetallic MOFs with different ratios of $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}/\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ are shown in Figure S27 (Supporting Information).
Notably, $\mathrm{Cu}_{1.3}\mathrm{Ni}_{1.7}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ not only has an excellent RL value of $-71.5$ dB at $12.16\mathrm{GHz}$ , but also reaches −42 dB at $9.36\:\mathrm{GHz}$ (Xband: $8{\mathrm{-}}12\ \mathrm{GHz}\$ ), and $^{-48}$ dB at $6.48~\mathrm{GHz}$ (C-band: $4{-}8\operatorname{GHz}$ ) (Figure 6c). With the increase in thickness, the absorption peak tends to be shift to the low-frequency region, which is consistent with the geometric and interference effects described by the quarter-wavelength theory.[40] When $\mathrm{t_{m}}$ and $\mathrm{f_{m}}$ conform to Equation S1 (Supporting Information), the incident wave and the reflected wave at the air-absorber interface have a phase difference of $180^{\circ}$ , which can completely eliminate the reflected electromagnetic wave and achieve the best electromagnetic wave absorption capacity (Figure 6e).[41] This unique feature makes it a potential candidate for a switchable high-absorption EMW material over a wide range $(4{-}18\operatorname{GHz})$ .
Coincidentally, with the increase of Cu element content, the position of the maximum absorption peak shows a tendency to move to the low-frequency region (Figure 6d), which we speculate may be the synergistic self-polarization effect of Ni and $\mathtt{C u}$ ions.[42] It is found that in bimetallic MOFs, the migration of free electrons is suppressed when the proportion of Cu ions is increased, which is attributed to the fact that the 3d orbit of Cu ions tend to obtain a fully filled state, making it difficult for electron transfer to occur, resulting in fewer active sites.[43] While the unstable valence electron structure of nickel ions enhances the electron transfer between copper ions and the strong coupling effect between the two metals, which also explains the phenomenon of peak shift at different metal ratios. It means that we can adjust the specific frequency of EMW absorption by molecular design. For example, we can increase the content of $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ to achieve a shift from high frequency to low frequency to better meet the actual needs. Certainly, whether such a conclusion is universal remains to be proven by future research. Nevertheless, this concept provides ideas for the absorption of specific frequencies of electromagnetic waves.
Based on the above discussion, we propose a plausible EMW absorption mechanism using $\mathrm{Cu}_{1.3}\mathrm{Ni}_{1.7}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ as an example. The inherent properties of the material endow it with multiple EMW attenuation loss mechanisms (Figure 6f): i) Due to its longrange ordered 2D conjugated conductive structure, electrons can migrate through space not only in the 2D planes along chemical bonds, but also in the $\boldsymbol{\mathscr{c}}$ -stacked layers, which endows the material with excellent electrical conductivity, meaning with higher conduction loss. ii) The high specific surface area and unique porous structure have enhanced scattering effect and polarization effect, which means more reflection channels, higher reflection frequencies, and larger polarization sites, which are beneficial for multi-polarization loss. iii) The co-synergies of the bimetals not only enhance the multi-polarizations effect and induces the formation of a microstructure more suitable for EMW absorption but also improve the electrical conductivity, leading to more abundant magnetic loss and further optimizing the EMW absorption performance. More importantly, the dielectric conduction loss demonstrates the high correlation with the metal ratio of these MOFs. The precise atomic coordination by molecular level can achieve a regulation of conductivity from $10^{-4}$ to $55.4~\mathrm{S~cm^{-1}}$ as reported in the literature,[19] which makes it possible to establish a wide range of dielectric database based on the pristine MOFs. Ideally, with the continuous exploration and improvement of the conductive MOFs database, future researchers can achieve customized functionalization of MAMs through precise selection.
## 3. Conclusion
A series of intrinsically conductive MOFs, $\mathbf{M}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{M_{x}M_{\bar{3}-x}^{\prime}(H I T P)_{2}}$ (M, $\scriptstyle\mathbf{M}^{\prime}=\mathbf{C}_{}0$ , Ni, Cu), have been successfully synthesized and directly applied to electromagnetic absorption for the first time. The bimetallic alloying strategy can not only contribute to the formation of a microstructure that is more conductive to the multiple reflection and scattering of electromagnetic waves, but also enhance the polarization effect to achieve multiple polarization losses. Moreover, the electromagnetic parameters and the absorption peak position can be precisely controlled by fine-tuning the ratio between metals at the molecular level, which greatly improves the EMW-absorbing properties of the bimetallic MOFs. The pristine bimetallic MOF, $\mathrm{Cu}_{1.3}\mathrm{Ni}_{1.7}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ , features an ultra-high absorption strength $(\mathrm{RL}~=~-71.5\$ dB) and an ultra-wide absorption bandwidth $(6.16~\mathrm{GHz})$ , that completely covers the entire Ku-band $(12-$ $^{18\mathrm{~GHz})}$ . By changing the thickness, the absorption peaks of $\mathrm{Cu}_{1.3}\mathrm{Ni}_{1.7}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ are switchable in a wide electromagnetic wave range $(4{-}18\operatorname{GHz})$ . Notably, the performance of $\mathrm{\tilde{\cu}}_{1.3}\mathrm{Ni}_{1.7}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ is in the top range of reported EMW absorbing materials. This work expands the application prospects of intrinsic MOFs in the field of EMW absorption, and lays a foundation for simplification, controllability, precision, and high efficiency of pristine MOFbased EMW absorbing materials, which is of milestone significance.
