Calculation of ${\bf\dot{C}}_{0}$ -value: The eddy current loss coefficient $\mathrm{C}_{0}$ can be calculated following Equation (3) to determine whether the EMW loss mechanism of the sample includes eddy current loss. According to the skin effect, if the magnetic loss only includes eddy current loss, $\mathbf{C}_{0}$ should be constant.
$$
C_{0}=\frac{\mu^{\prime\prime}}{\sqrt{\mu^{\prime}}f}
$$
Where $f$ represent the frequency of EMW.
Calculation of impedance matching $Z$ -value: The impedance matching value (Z) determine whether the EMW can be reflected on the surface or can enter the interior of the material. The closer the Z value is to 1, the smaller the impedance difference between the environment and the material, so that EMW can enter the material to a greater extent and reduce reflection on the surface.
$$
Z=\sqrt{\mu_{r}/\varepsilon_{r}}
$$
Calculation of attenuation constant $\alpha$ : The larger the $\alpha$ , the stronger the absorption ability of the incident electromagnetic wave
$$
\begin{array}{c}{{\alpha=\displaystyle\frac{\sqrt{2}\pi f}{c}\times\sqrt{\left(\mu^{\prime\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}-\mu^{\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime}\right)}}}\\ {{+\sqrt{\left(\mu^{\prime\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}-\mu^{\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime}\right)^{2}+\left(\mu^{\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}-\mu^{\prime\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime}\right)^{2}}}}\end{array}
$$
Where $c$ represent the speed of light in free space.
Debye relaxation theory: Usually, each semicircle represents a relaxation polarization process and the number of the semicircles reflects the intensity of relaxed polarization.
$$
\left(\varepsilon^{\prime}-\frac{\varepsilon_{s}+\varepsilon_{\infty}}{2}\right)^{2}+\left(\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}\right)^{2}=\left(\frac{\varepsilon_{s}-\varepsilon_{\infty}}{2}\right)^{2}
$$
Where $\epsilon_{\mathrm{s}}$ is the intrinsic permittivity, $\epsilon_{\infty}$ is the relative permittivity at the limit of high frequency.
